    A('In the lecture’s cost function J(x) = [y − F(x)]ᵀS_y⁻¹[y − F(x)] + [x − xₐ]ᵀR[x − xₐ], what does the second term do?',
      'It is the constraint towards the a-priori profile that makes the ill-posed problem solvable', ['It weights the measurement errors by the covariance of the observations', 'It is the radiative transfer model F evaluated at the a-priori state', 'It converts the retrieved profile into a total column'],
      'Slide 115: the first term is the weighted spectral misfit (S_y = error covariance of the observations, F = radiative transfer model); the second is the constraint R toward a priori knowledge because the problem is ill-posed.', 2, 'Cost function', origin='goc', src=SRC + ' slide 115')
    A('Why is an iterative solution used for the retrieval?',
      'The forward model F(x) is non-linear in x, so the Jacobian K must be recomputed at each iterate xᵢ', ['Because the averaging kernel changes with the true profile only', 'Because the a-priori profile is unknown and must be guessed repeatedly', 'Because the satellite measures a different spectrum at each iteration'],
      'Slide 115: “Iterative solution (non-linearity)”: x_(i+1) = xᵢ + (KᵢᵀS_e⁻¹Kᵢ + C)⁻¹[KᵢᵀS_e⁻¹(y − F(xᵢ)) − C(xᵢ − xₐ)].', 3, 'Cost function', origin='goc', src=SRC + ' slide 115')
